Pi Scholar — third-party attribution

The following projects informed Pi Scholar's product policies and acceptance
fixtures. The references below are pinned for provenance; they are not runtime
dependencies and their repositories are not read at runtime.

- pi-llm-wiki, https://github.com/zosmaai/pi-llm-wiki, revision
  a4c9da4b4694: Pi-native knowledge interaction, strict OKF parsing, Markdown
  links, deterministic projections, and guardrails.
- Engram, https://github.com/nagisanzenin/engram, revision d0a61cd67130:
  retrieval practice, distributed practice, topic interleaving, isolated
  assessment, and FSRS/evidence policies.
- Cribrum, https://github.com/N-F9/cribrum-lite, revision f54da48676c7:
  safe admission, lossless atomization, bounded wiki-to-quiz workflows,
  process containment, locks, cadence, and Git synchronization semantics.

Pi Scholar does not ship those projects, their data, or their storage/runtime
homes. This file records design and behavioral attribution only; no donor source
file is copied into this package. Any future adapted donor code must retain the
corresponding upstream license and attribution text here before release.

Direct runtime dependencies used by this package include:

- @mariozechner/pi-coding-agent — MIT, loaded as a Pi peer package.
- typebox — MIT, loaded as a Pi peer package.
- ts-fsrs — see its installed package LICENSE and upstream notices.
- mdast-util-from-markdown — MIT, used to identify standard Markdown code spans and blocks during provenance parsing.
- yaml — ISC, used for OKF v0.2 frontmatter parsing and serialization.
- remark-math 6.0.0 — MIT, used to parse inline and display math in Markdown.
- mdast-util-math 3.0.0 and micromark-extension-math 3.1.0 — MIT, used to parse the same math nodes before quiz metadata-leak validation.
- rehype-katex 7.0.1 — MIT, used to render parsed math with KaTeX.
- katex 0.16.25 — MIT, used to typeset mathematical notation in the web interface.
- rehype-highlight 7.0.2 — MIT, used to add syntax-highlight tokens to fenced code.
